# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the SPURS scheduler fixtures.
#
# Guest memory is shared, unsynchronised memory by design: guest code (here the
# manufactured policies and the fake SPU producer) reads and writes it with
# plain accesses, and the scheduler's first look at a request byte is a
# lock-free peek that is re-read under spu_lockline_lock before anything is
# consumed. Only accesses made through the guest-memory accessors are listed,
# so a race on host scheduler state (locks, lanes, counters) still reports.
race:vm_read8
race:vm_read16
race:vm_read32
race:vm_write8
race:wkl_count_get
race:wkl_count_set
race:spu_raise_ready
# Pre-existing lazy initialisation in the SPU runtime, unrelated to SPURS
# admission (racy first-use flags that settle to one value).
race:yz_lockstep_enabled
race:spu_direct_init
